---
title: "Install packages"
weight: 5
draft: false
---

Before you can analyse any text, you need to set up R and a handful of add-on packages. Here is everything you need to install, in the order you need it. If you already have R, an IDE and **quanteda** installed and working, feel free to skip ahead to the next chapter.

## R and your IDE

An IDE (integrated development environment) bundles the editor, console and debugger you need to write and run code into one program. You need three layers: **R** itself does the work, an **IDE** is where you write code and inspect objects, and **packages** such as **quanteda** add the functions you need. [Base R](https://cran.r-project.org/) is required regardless of which IDE you pick, since the IDE is just a front end for it.

For the IDE layer, [RStudio](https://www.rstudio.com/products/rstudio/download/) is where most R users start. [Positron](https://positron.posit.co/) is a newer editor from the same company, built on VS Code, with similar support for R and Python side by side. If you already use [VS Code](https://code.visualstudio.com/), you can add R support via an extension instead of switching editors. None of these choices changes how **quanteda** works, so pick whichever fits how you already work.

Whichever IDE you choose, open your work as a project or folder rather than individual files: File > New/Open Project in RStudio, or File > Open Folder in Positron and VS Code. This sets your working directory automatically, so commands like `read.csv("data.csv")` find files without full paths, and scripts keep working when you move the project to another computer.

## Quanteda

A "package" in R is a bundle of ready-made functions that someone else has written, so that you do not have to write everything from scratch. **quanteda** is the package that does the heavy lifting in these tutorials: it turns raw text into objects that R can count, compare and model. You only need to install a package once on your computer, but you need to load it with `library()` every time you start a new R session.

You need to have **quanteda** installed. If your IDE has a menu for installing packages, you can use that, or run the following command directly in the R console.

```{r, eval=FALSE}
install.packages("quanteda")
```

Since the release of **quanteda** version 3.0, `textstat_*`, `textmodel_*` and `textplot_*` functions (for statistics, models and plots) are available in separate packages rather than in **quanteda** itself, which keeps the core package smaller and faster to install. We will use several of these functions in the chapters below. We recommend installing these packages now, so you do not have to stop and install them halfway through a later tutorial.

```{r, eval=FALSE}
install.packages("quanteda.textmodels")
install.packages("quanteda.textstats")
install.packages("quanteda.textplots")
```

{{% notice info %}}
If you use the **quanteda** package in your research, please cite:
Benoit, Kenneth, Kohei Watanabe, Haiyan Wang, Paul Nulty, Adam Obeng, Stefan Müller, and Akitaka Matsuo. 2018. "quanteda: An R Package for the Quantitative Analysis of Textual Data." *Journal of Open Source Software* 3 (30): 774. <https://doi.org/10.21105/joss.00774>.
{{% /notice %}}


## Other packages

**quanteda** itself expects text that is already loaded into R. To get text out of files on your computer, such as `.csv`, `.txt` or `.pdf` files, and into R, we will use the **readtext** package throughout these tutorials. Again, you can use your IDE's package-install menu, or run the following command.

```{r, eval=FALSE}
install.packages("readtext")
```

Several tutorials also use example datasets, such as collections of newspaper articles or political speeches, that are too large to ship inside **quanteda** itself. These live in a companion package called **quanteda.corpora**. Unlike the packages above, it is not published on CRAN (the usual place R downloads packages from), so install it directly from its code on GitHub with the `install_github()` function from the **devtools** package.

```{r, eval=FALSE}
install.packages("devtools") # get devtools to install quanteda.corpora
devtools::install_github("quanteda/quanteda.corpora")
```


{{% notice note %}}
If you already have **quanteda** and other packages installed, run Tools > Check for Package Updates to install the latest versions. We recommend updating all the packages using `update.packages()` to avoid errors caused by dependencies.
{{% /notice %}}


## Additional packages

The tutorials do not cover syntactical analysis, but you should install **spacyr** for part-of-speech tagging, entity recognition, and dependency parsing. **spacyr** provides an interface to the spaCy library and works well with **quanteda**. Note that you need to have Python installed to use the **spacyr** package. See the [package description](https://github.com/quanteda/spacyr/blob/master/README.md) for more information.

```{r, eval=FALSE}
install.packages("spacyr")
```

Finally, in the tutorials we will show how to use **newsmap** to [classify documents](https://tutorials.quanteda.io/machine-learning/newsmap/) based on "seed words", the **wordvector** package to [construct word embeddings](https://tutorials.quanteda.io/machine-learning/content/machine-learning/embeddings.en.markdown) the **seededlda** package to run topic models. You can download the packages from [CRAN](https://cran.r-project.org/package=newsmap).

```{r, eval=FALSE}
install.packages("newsmap")
install.packages("seededlda")
install.packages("wordvector")
```

Once a package is installed, R does not load it automatically. Every time you start a new R session, you need to tell R which packages you want to use in that session with `require()` or `library()`. To sum up, you need to load the following packages to run all examples in this tutorial:

```{r, eval=FALSE}
library(quanteda)
library(quanteda.textmodels)
library(quanteda.textstats)
library(quanteda.textplots)
library(readtext)
library(devtools)
library(quanteda.corpora)
library(newsmap)
library(seededlda)
library(wordvector)
```

{{% notice note %}}
With `quanteda_options()` you can get or set global options affecting functions across **quanteda**. One useful feature is changing the number of threads to use in parallelised functions. By default, **quanteda** uses all threads available on your machine, but you can restrict this, for example to leave capacity for other programs.
For instance, `quanteda_options("threads" = 2)` will limit parallelised functions to two threads.
{{% /notice %}}

